  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0066\" consists of 7 tables. The main purpose of this schema is to manage various aspects of a software application, including user management, role-based access control, notifications, attachments, and application settings.\nThe \"attachmentable\" table is used to link various entities in the system to attachments. It has four columns: 'id', 'attachmentable_type', 'attachmentable_id', and 'attachment_id'. The 'attachment_id' column is a foreign key referencing the 'id' column in the 'attachments' table.\nThe \"attachments\" table is used to store information about files that are attached to various entities in the system. It has nine columns: 'id', 'name', 'original_name', 'mime', 'extension', 'size', 'sort', 'path', 'description', 'alt', 'hash', 'disk', 'user_id', 'group', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'.\nThe \"notifications\" table is used to store information about notifications that are sent to users. It has eight columns: 'id', 'type', 'notifiable_type', 'notifiable_id', 'data', 'read_at', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'. The 'notifiable_id' column is a foreign key referencing the 'id' column in the 'users' table.\nThe \"role_users\" table is used to manage the relationship between users and roles. It has two columns: 'user_id' and 'role_id'. Both columns are foreign keys referencing the 'id' column in the 'users' and 'roles' tables respectively.\nThe \"roles\" table is used to store information about roles. It has six columns: 'id', 'slug', 'name', 'permissions', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'.\nThe \"settings\" table is used to store key-value pairs of settings. It has two columns: 'key' and 'value'.\nFinally, the \"users\" table is used to store information about users. It has eleven columns: 'id', 'name', 'email', 'email_verified_at', 'password', 'remember_token', 'created_at', 'updated_at', 'last_login', and 'permissions'.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an administration platform using Laravel, leveraging Orchid—a comprehensive solution for creating admin panels and back-office applications. This platform will serve as a foundation for building content management systems and line-of-business applications with rapid and efficient development.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary goal is to enable the creation of robust administration applications by providing a framework that emphasizes code-driven development. The system should facilitate efficient handling of UI components, user permissions, and file attachments.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Use the platform to build and customize admin applications.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles, permissions, and content.\n- End Users: Access application features according to assigned roles and permissions.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- UI Components: Provide an extensive array of UI elements such as form inputs, dialogs, data grids, and visualizations. Ensure these components can be extended and composed directly in the code.\n  \n- Permissions Management: Facilitate seamless management of user roles and permissions through an intuitive interface, ensuring application security without the need for complex coding.\n\n- File Attachments: Enable attaching various file formats to application records, supporting organized data management and streamlined workflow for users.\n\n- Role Management: Implement a robust mechanism for users to be assigned multiple roles, linking users with roles and permissions as defined in the `roles` table and its relationships.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: Ensure the platform can handle efficient creation and management of admin panels without significant latencies, leveraging Laravel's inherent capabilities.\n\n- Scalability: Design the system to support scalable growth in terms of number of users and attached files, ensuring the underlying architecture supports expansion.\n\n- Security: Enforce strong security measures, particularly in permissions management, to protect sensitive data and operations.\n\n- Usability: Offer a straightforward and intuitive interface for developers and administrators, facilitating ease of use in building and managing applications.\n\n- Reliability: Ensure data integrity and consistent performance, especially concerning the attachment, storage, and retrieval of files as well as notification handling.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0066.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0066\nCREATE TABLE `attachments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `original_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `mime` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `extension` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `size` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `sort` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `path` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `alt` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `hash` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `disk` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `group` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `user_id` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `attachments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `attachmentable` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `attachmentable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `attachmentable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `attachment_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `attachment_id` (`attachment_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `attachmentable_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`attachment_id`) REFERENCES `attachments` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `data` text NOT NULL,\n  `read_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `notifiable_id` (`notifiable_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `notifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`notifiable_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `role_users` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`, `role_id`),\n  KEY `role_id` (`role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `role_users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `role_users_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `slug` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permissions` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `settings` (\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`key`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email_verified_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `permissions` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
